---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Doc from '../components/legal/Doc.astro';
const updated = '29 October 2026';
// Drafts stay out of search until counsel signs off and every placeholder is filled (design/legal/README.md).
const draft = true;
---

<Base title="Privacy policy · Comarket" description="What Comarket collects, why, who processes it, how long we keep it, and how to use your rights." stage="none" noindex={draft}>
  <Doc
    title="Privacy policy"
    lede="What we collect, why, who helps us, how long we keep it, and how to see, change or delete it."
    updated={updated}
    draft={draft}
    current="/privacy"
  >
    <div class="short">
      <b>The short version</b>
      <ul>
        <li>Today this site runs a waitlist. We keep what you type into it, a few facts about how you found us, and, if you allow it, a random visitor ID so we can tell which pages work.</li>
        <li>We don't sell your personal information, share it for cross-site advertising, or use third-party ad trackers.</li>
        <li>In the EU, EEA, UK and Switzerland we ask before setting any analytics cookie. Everywhere else you can turn analytics off on the <a href="/cookies">cookie page</a>.</li>
        <li>Email <a href="mailto:[[PRIVACY EMAIL]]">[[PRIVACY EMAIL]]</a> to see, correct or delete your data. We answer within one month.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h2>Who we are</h2>
    <p>
      Comarket is run by [[LEGAL ENTITY NAME]], a [[ENTITY TYPE AND JURISDICTION]] registered under number [[COMPANY NUMBER]],
      with its registered office at [[REGISTERED ADDRESS]] ("Comarket", "we", "us"). For the personal information described
      here we are the controller, except where this policy says we act for a brand (see "When we act for a brand").
    </p>
    <p>
      Contact for anything in this policy: <a href="mailto:[[PRIVACY EMAIL]]">[[PRIVACY EMAIL]]</a>, or post to the address
      above. Data protection officer: [[DPO CONTACT]]. If we are established outside the EU, our representative in the EU
      under Article 27 GDPR is [[EU REPRESENTATIVE]]. If we are established outside the UK, our UK representative is
      [[UK REPRESENTATIVE]].
    </p>

    <h2>What this policy covers</h2>
    <p>
      This policy covers comarket.si and its subdomains, the waitlist, and the emails we send. Comarket matches brands with
      creators and gives each creator a co-branded storefront page. That platform has not opened yet. The section "When the
      platform opens" describes what it will collect, so that people joining the waitlist know what comes next. We will
      update this policy and tell waitlist members before the platform goes live.
    </p>

    <h2>What we collect on this site, and why</h2>
    <p>
      The table lists each purpose, the information used, our legal basis under the GDPR and UK GDPR, and how long we keep it.
      "Legitimate interests" means we have weighed our interest against yours. You can ask us for that assessment, and you can
      object (see "Your rights").
    </p>
    <div class="tbl">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Purpose</th><th>Information</th><th>Legal basis</th><th>Kept for</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Holding your place on the waitlist and telling you when it opens</td>
            <td>Email; whether you are a brand or a creator; your brand's website and name (brands) or your handle and platform (creators); your pass number; the date you joined</td>
            <td>Steps you asked us to take before a contract (Art. 6(1)(b))</td>
            <td>Until you ask us to remove it, or 24 months after you joined if you have not opened an account by then</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Honouring the price a brand was shown</td>
            <td>The pricing version and fee rates shown to you when you joined (brands only)</td>
            <td>Steps before a contract (Art. 6(1)(b)) and legitimate interests in keeping our word</td>
            <td>As long as the waitlist entry, then for the life of any Brand Agreement</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Knowing how people found us</td>
            <td>Campaign tags in the link you followed (utm_ tags), the page that sent you (referrer), your country as reported by our host, and the pass number in a referral link (<code>?ref=</code>)</td>
            <td>Legitimate interests in measuring our marketing and crediting referrals</td>
            <td>As long as the waitlist entry</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Optional questions after you join (for example what you sell, how you charge, your audience size or topic)</td>
            <td>Your answers, linked to your pass</td>
            <td>Consent (Art. 6(1)(a)), which you can withdraw at any time</td>
            <td>As long as the waitlist entry, or until you withdraw consent</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>The confirmation email</td>
            <td>Email, pass number, brand name or handle</td>
            <td>Steps before a contract (Art. 6(1)(b))</td>
            <td>Our email provider keeps delivery logs for a limited period under its own settings</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Building a storefront preview when a brand types its website</td>
            <td>The website you type, and the public name, theme color, icon and share image on its homepage, which our server fetches</td>
            <td>Legitimate interests in showing you what your storefront would look like</td>
            <td>24 hours in a cache, not linked to you; kept with your entry only if you join</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Checking whether a creator handle is free</td>
            <td>The handle you type</td>
            <td>Legitimate interests</td>
            <td>Not stored unless you join</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Analytics: learning which pages and versions work</td>
            <td>A random visitor ID (cookie), the page version you were shown, and simple events such as which side you chose, whether you built a preview or used a calculator, and whether you saved or shared your pass</td>
            <td>Consent in the EU, EEA, UK and Switzerland (Art. 6(1)(a) and the ePrivacy and PECR cookie rules); legitimate interests elsewhere, with an opt-out</td>
            <td>13 months, then deleted or reduced to totals that identify no one</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Testing prices and pages on a share of visitors</td>
            <td>Which version you were assigned, linked to your visitor ID</td>
            <td>As for analytics</td>
            <td>13 months</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Keeping the site secure and stopping bots</td>
            <td>IP address, browser and device signals, and request logs, handled by Cloudflare (including its Turnstile bot check on the waitlist form)</td>
            <td>Legitimate interests in security; the bot check is strictly necessary for the form</td>
            <td>Request logs up to 7 days. We don't store your IP address in our database</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Answering you when you write to us</td>
            <td>Your message, email and anything you include</td>
            <td>Legitimate interests, or steps before a contract</td>
            <td>24 months after the conversation ends</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Meeting legal duties and defending claims</td>
            <td>What the duty or claim needs</td>
            <td>Legal obligation (Art. 6(1)(c)) and legitimate interests</td>
            <td>As long as the law requires or the claim lasts</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      You don't have to give us anything to read this site. To join the waitlist we need your email and your website or
      handle; without them we can't hold your place. The questions after you join are optional.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you save your pass image, it is made in your browser and stays on your device. If you use the share button, you go
      to X (formerly Twitter), and X's own privacy policy applies to what you post there.
    </p>

    <h2>When the platform opens</h2>
    <p>This section describes what the platform will process. It is not live yet.</p>
    <h3>Brands</h3>
    <p>
      Account and contact details for the people who run your account; your company and billing details; your offers,
      creator rates and approvals; and, through the Stripe account you connect, the information we need to decide whether a
      sale counts and to bill you. Legal basis: performance of our contract with your company (Art. 6(1)(b)) for the account,
      legitimate interests for the individuals who work for it, and legal obligation for invoices and tax records.
    </p>
    <h3>Creators</h3>
    <p>
      Your account details, profile, handle, the public social profiles you link and their public statistics (such as
      follower counts), the offers you accept, your storefront and hub content, your sales and earnings, and the identity,
      tax and payout details that Stripe and the law require, which may include your legal name, address, date of birth, tax
      identification number and VAT number. Stripe collects and holds your bank details; we don't see your full account
      number. Legal basis: performance of our contract with you (Art. 6(1)(b)); legal obligation for tax reporting, such as
      the EU's DAC7 rules and US information returns (Art. 6(1)(c)); legitimate interests for fraud prevention.
    </p>
    <h3>Shoppers who visit a storefront</h3>
    <p>
      When you open a creator's storefront, we record which storefront you came from and when, so the brand can credit the
      creator. When you go on to the brand's checkout, the brand processes your purchase under its own privacy policy. To
      decide whether the sale counts, we compare your email, phone number and a card fingerprint (a code Stripe derives from
      your card, never the card number) with the brand's existing Stripe customers. We do that on the brand's behalf, as its
      processor (see the next section). We do not sell shopper data, and we never show a creator who bought.
    </p>

    <h2>When we act for a brand</h2>
    <p>
      For the new-customer check and the sale reports we prepare for a brand, the brand is the controller and we are its
      processor, under our <a href="/dpa">data processing addendum</a>. If you bought from a brand and want to use your rights
      over that purchase data, contact the brand; we will help it answer. We remain the controller for our own records of
      each counted sale (a reference number, dates, amounts and the creator credited), which we keep to bill the brand, pay the
      creator, meet tax rules and handle disputes.
    </p>

    <h2>Automated decisions</h2>
    <p>
      On the platform, software decides whether a sale counts: whether the buyer is new to the brand, whether the payment
      cleared, whether it was refunded in time, and whether there are signs of fraud, such as a creator buying through their
      own link. These decisions affect what a creator is paid and what a brand is billed. Anyone affected can ask for a person
      to review a decision, explain their side and contest it, by writing to [[CONTACT EMAIL]]. We tell creators why a sale did
      not count, without revealing who the buyer was.
    </p>

    <h2>Who we share it with</h2>
    <p>
      We use the service providers below to run the site. They process personal information only on our instructions and
      under contracts that require them to protect it.
    </p>
    <div class="tbl">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Provider</th><th>What they do for us</th><th>Information</th><th>Where</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Cloudflare, Inc.</td>
            <td>Hosts the site and runs our server code (Workers), caches previews (KV), checks for bots (Turnstile), connects to our database (Hyperdrive), and keeps short request logs</td>
            <td>Everything sent to the site, including IP address and device signals</td>
            <td>Global network; company in the United States</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Neon</td>
            <td>Stores the waitlist, page-version assignments and analytics events in a Postgres database</td>
            <td>Waitlist entries, answers, visitor IDs and events</td>
            <td>[[NEON REGION]]</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Resend</td>
            <td>Sends the confirmation email</td>
            <td>Email, pass number, brand name or handle</td>
            <td>[[RESEND REGION]]</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>[[SUPPORT EMAIL PROVIDER]]</td>
            <td>Hosts our inbox</td>
            <td>Messages you send us</td>
            <td>[[SUPPORT EMAIL REGION]]</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Stripe (when the platform opens)</td>
            <td>Brand billing, creator payouts, identity checks and tax forms. We also read the brand's own Stripe account, with the brand's permission</td>
            <td>Billing, payout and tax details; sale data</td>
            <td>United States, Ireland and other Stripe locations</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>We also share personal information:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Between brands and creators, once the platform opens.</b> A brand sees the profile and public statistics of creators it is matched with, and their sales results. A creator sees the brand's offer. Neither sees a shopper's identity through Comarket.</li>
      <li><b>When the law requires it,</b> or to protect people, our users or Comarket from fraud or harm, and to establish or defend legal claims.</li>
      <li><b>In a sale or reorganisation of our business,</b> with the buyer or successor, who must respect this policy. We will tell you first.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      We don't sell personal information, and we don't share it for cross-context behavioural advertising, as California law
      uses those terms. We have not done either in the past 12 months.
    </p>

    <h2>International transfers</h2>
    <p>
      Some providers above process information outside the UK, the EU and the EEA, mainly in the United States. Where a
      country has no adequacy decision, we rely on the European Commission's standard contractual clauses, the UK's
      International Data Transfer Addendum to them, and the Swiss version of the clauses. Where a provider is certified under
      the EU-US Data Privacy Framework and its UK and Swiss extensions, we may rely on that instead. You can ask us for a copy
      of the relevant safeguards at [[PRIVACY EMAIL]].
    </p>

    <h2>How long we keep it</h2>
    <p>
      The table above gives the period for each purpose. When a period ends we delete the information or reduce it to totals
      that identify no one. On the platform, account information lasts as long as the account and then [[ACCOUNT RETENTION]];
      invoices, payout records and the records tax law requires last [[TAX RECORD RETENTION]]; and records of counted sales
      last until their commission period ends, then through the refund and dispute periods that follow and the tax period.
    </p>

    <h2>How we protect it</h2>
    <p>
      Traffic to the site is encrypted. The database is reached only by our server code, through credentials kept as secrets.
      Analytics use a random ID, not your name or IP address. Access to personal information is limited to the people who
      need it. No system is perfectly secure; if a breach puts your rights at risk, we will tell you and the regulator as the
      law requires.
    </p>

    <h2>Your rights</h2>
    <p>Depending on where you live, you can ask us to:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>tell you whether we hold information about you and give you a copy (access);</li>
      <li>correct it (rectification);</li>
      <li>delete it (erasure), for example by leaving the waitlist;</li>
      <li>limit what we do with it while a question is resolved (restriction);</li>
      <li>give it to you, or to another company, in a common machine-readable format (portability);</li>
      <li>stop using it where we rely on legitimate interests, including for analytics (objection); and</li>
      <li>withdraw consent at any time, without affecting what we did before.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Email <a href="mailto:[[PRIVACY EMAIL]]">[[PRIVACY EMAIL]]</a>, ideally from the address you signed up with, so we can
      confirm it's you. We may ask for a little more to verify you, and nothing more than that. We answer within one month,
      or tell you within that month if we need up to two more months for a complex request. Requests are free unless they are
      clearly unfounded or excessive.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can also complain to a data protection authority, in particular where you live or work. Our lead authority is
      [[LEAD SUPERVISORY AUTHORITY]]. In the UK it is the Information Commissioner's Office (ico.org.uk). We would like the
      chance to put things right first.
    </p>

    <h2>US state privacy rights</h2>
    <p>
      This section is our notice for residents of California and other US states with consumer privacy laws. We give the
      rights below to every user in the US, whether or not a particular state law applies to us yet.
    </p>
    <p><b>What we collected in the past 12 months, by the categories California law uses:</b></p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Identifiers:</b> email address, creator handle, visitor ID, pass number.</li>
      <li><b>Commercial information:</b> the fee rates shown to a brand; on the platform, sales and earnings records.</li>
      <li><b>Internet or other electronic network activity:</b> pages and versions viewed, events, referrer, campaign tags.</li>
      <li><b>Geolocation data:</b> country only, from your IP address. Not precise location.</li>
      <li><b>Professional or employment-related information:</b> your brand's website and name, your platform and audience details.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Sources, purposes and retention are in the tables above. We disclose these categories to the service providers listed,
      for the business purposes described. We don't sell or share personal information, and we don't knowingly sell or share
      the information of anyone under 16. On the platform we will collect creators' tax and identity information, which
      California treats as sensitive (such as a tax ID); we use it only to pay creators and meet the law, never to infer
      anything about them.
    </p>
    <p>
      <b>Your rights:</b> to know what we collect, use and disclose; to get a copy; to delete; to correct; to opt out of sale or
      sharing (which we don't do); to limit the use of sensitive information (we already limit it); and not to be treated
      differently for using any of them. To make a request, email <a href="mailto:[[PRIVACY EMAIL]]">[[PRIVACY EMAIL]]</a>. An
      authorised agent can make a request for you with your signed permission; we may ask you to confirm your identity
      directly. If we refuse a request, you can appeal by replying to our answer. If the appeal fails, you can contact your
      state attorney general.
    </p>
    <p>We treat a Global Privacy Control signal from your browser as a request to turn analytics off for that browser.</p>

    <h2>Cookies</h2>
    <p>
      We set a small number of first-party cookies, listed with their purposes and lifetimes in our
      <a href="/cookies">cookie policy</a>, where you can also change your choice. We don't use advertising cookies,
      third-party analytics or social media pixels, and our fonts are served from our own site.
    </p>

    <h2>Emails</h2>
    <p>
      Joining the waitlist sends one confirmation email, and we email once more when your spot opens. We send other news only
      if you agree to it, and every such email has a way to stop. We don't sell or rent email addresses.
    </p>

    <h2>Children</h2>
    <p>
      This site is not for children. The waitlist, the Brand Agreement and the Creator Agreement are for adults (18 or older)
      acting for a business or for their own creator work. We don't knowingly collect personal information from anyone under
      16. If you think a child has given us information, write to [[PRIVACY EMAIL]] and we will delete it.
    </p>

    <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
    <p>
      We will post any change here with a new date. If a change matters to how we use information we already hold, we will
      email the people affected before it applies and, where the law requires, ask for consent again.
    </p>
  </Doc>
  <Footer />
</Base>
